+## Delivered follow-up: district directory and map story panel
+
+The discovery map now uses a **1:2 desktop layout**: district directory on the left, existing Leaflet map on the right. Selecting a curated place from a map pin or district entry replaces the directory with a video, place title, description and detail-page link. Back restores the directory and stops the video; selecting another place replaces the previous player. Search/category/distance filters apply to directory links as well as pins; an excluded selection closes. The layout stacks on smaller screens. The illustrated fallback also opens the story panel, while destination links remain available without JavaScript.
+
+**Content scope:** the directory contains 30 former district-level areas (12 urban districts, 17 rural districts and Sơn Tây), explicitly labeled historical rather than current administrative units. [Hanoi's official account of the July 2025 administrative change](https://hanoi.gov.vn/videos/ha-noi-cong-bo-nghi-quyet-sap-xep-don-vi-hanh-chinh-van-hanh-chinh-quyen-hai-cap-tu-1-7-2025-425063022371804.htm) and the [former 30-area arrangement](https://quydautuphattrien.hanoi.gov.vn/ban-tin/phuong-an-chi-tiet-sap-xep-30-quan-huyen-thi-xa-cua-ha-noi-380.html) were consulted October 16, 2026. Only the existing six destinations are mapped; empty districts are marked as having no curated content. This is an editorial directory, not district-boundary mapping or a complete places database. Each story uses the existing [Vietnam Tourism My Ha Noi overview](https://vietnam.travel/things-to-do/my-hanoi), labeled as a city overview, not destination-specific footage. Destination-specific video production/selection remains unquoted future content work.
+
+**Recommendation and decision:** retain the prototype's Leaflet/public OSM tiles and standard YouTube player; no Mapbox account, paid geocoding, boundary API, routing API, CMS or new dependency was introduced. Incremental configured recurring vendor subtotal is **USD $0/month before applicable tax**, assuming the same static hosting and standard embedding setup. No new subscription, Data API calls, backend compute, database, video-storage product, image service or transaction fees are configured. The HTML adds six inert story templates: video requests begin only when a story is selected, with at most one map-story player attached at a time. This is in addition to the separate three editorial players, whose earlier forecasts remain applicable.
+
+The [primary OSM tile policy](https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/tiles/) and [YouTube embedding guide](https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/171780?hl=en) were rechecked October 16, 2026. OSM offers no published numeric tile allowance or commercial availability guarantee; access can be blocked without notice rather than trigger a paid automatic upgrade. Browser caching, visible attribution, HTTPS and referrer headers remain; no offline download/prefetch was added. The guide supports the standard/privacy-enhanced player and warns of owner-disabled embeds, age restrictions and required referrer headers. Neither source supplies a new metered unit-price quote for this setup; $0 is our configured vendor-fee estimate, not a guaranteed unlimited allowance. Existing YouTube commercial/privacy conditions above remain applicable; commercial rights, audience/jurisdiction and Vietnam playback availability need release review.
+
+**Updated map-interaction forecasts (unmeasured):** a map opening is one initialization, not a unique user. A tile request fetches one small map image. A story selection creates one video player; repeated selections count again even if media is cached. The narrower but taller viewport and pin-centering can alter tile demand. Conservatively retain 24 requests/opening for low/expected scenarios and 60 for heavy exploration, including assumed interaction; measure rather than treat these as quotas.
+
+| Scenario | Map openings/month | Tile requests/month | Assumed story selections/opening | Additional story-player loads/month | Configured map/video vendor subtotal/month |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Low | 1,000 | 1,000 × 24 = 24,000 | 0.25 | 1,000 × 0.25 = 250 | $0 |
+| Expected | 10,000 | 10,000 × 24 = 240,000 | 0.5 | 10,000 × 0.5 = 5,000 | $0 |
+| Heavy | 100,000 | 100,000 × 60 = 6,000,000 | 2 | 100,000 × 2 = 200,000 | $0, service can be blocked |
+
+These are demand assumptions, not measured bills or suitable guaranteed public-OSM production volumes. Player scripts, previews, ads and media segments add variable third-party requests/bytes. OSM and YouTube serve those assets; our host serves only the expanded static HTML/CSS/JavaScript. Public hosting/storage/bandwidth pricing and limit/overage behavior remain unknown until a host is selected. Domains, tax, currency conversion, implementation, video rights/production, content curation and maintenance labor remain excluded/unquoted. This subtotal is not the full business cost.
+
+The simpler alternative is the old full-width map linking to existing detail pages: less inline content/player demand, but not the requested browsing experience. A static directory/illustrated map avoids tile dependence but lacks live geographic detail. A paid map supplier is still a proposed reliability alternative requiring refreshed eligibility, Vietnam coverage and traffic-based pricing before commitment; no paid option is silently selected. Monitor actual tile counts, map/video errors and host transfer in a pilot. Keep the illustrated fallback, retry button, video viewing/source link and place-page link. Configure supported hosting/provider budget controls before paid deployment; alerts are not hard spending caps. No additional paid commitment is required for this prototype change.
+
+Source review and district-data tests were authored, but tests, application checks, browser interactions/playback, and PDF generation/visual inspection were not executed under the instance resource policy. On a capable development machine run `npm run test`, `npm run check`, `npm run build`, and `uv run --with reportlab python scripts/build-billing-brief.py`. Check live and fallback pin selection, keyboard activation/Back, filter changes, video teardown, empty districts, mobile stacking, preserved map attribution and the regenerated PDF. The PDF status above remains applicable.
+
+## Proposed: Contemporary Travel Editorial redesign — awaiting approval
+
+October 17, 2026: Phase A source/asset review proposes a lighter treatment of the existing incense photograph, Newsreader display typography with Be Vietnam Pro supporting text, an ivory/ink editorial system, rights-cleared video covers linking to the unchanged YouTube videos, and a restyled functional Leaflet discovery interface. No application code has been changed for this proposal. Browser inspection, screenshots and reproduction of the reported tile failure are unavailable in this session; this is not a visually verified implementation or confirmed map fix.
+
+**Recommendation and decision:** approve the visual direction before implementation; retain the static architecture and existing integrations. Do not purchase a tile plan or imagery on the strength of this estimate. Three story-specific editorial covers are missing; their acquisition/production and commercial rights are unquoted. No unrelated photographs should be substituted. Retain existing content, routes, data, filtering, geolocation, video URLs and map interactions.
+
+**Expected incremental recurring subscription/API subtotal: USD $0/month before applicable tax**, conditional on no new paid supplier, unchanged hosting contract and rights-cleared local assets. This is not the full business cost or a guarantee of unchanged hosting charges. One-time design/engineering, content licensing/production, maintenance, domains, hosting storage/transfer, taxes and currency conversion are excluded/unquoted. No backend compute, database, transactions or self-hosted video is proposed. Outbound video covers would remove the three editorial iframe loads; the map-story player remains unless separately approved. YouTube availability and third-party commercial terms still apply.
+
+| Unmeasured scenario | Monthly page loads | Added configured subscription/API fee | Additional local cover/font transfer |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| Low | 1,000 | $0 | 1,000 × B ÷ 1,000,000,000 GB |
+| Expected | 10,000 | $0 | 10,000 × B ÷ 1,000,000,000 GB |
+| Heavy | 100,000 | $0 | 100,000 × B ÷ 1,000,000,000 GB |
+
+B means the total additional cover/font bytes downloaded per uncached visit; final files are not selected, so no numeric bandwidth forecast is defensible yet. Caching and responsive image selection change actual transfer. Local fonts trade external font connections for our host's storage/transfer; they do not eliminate bandwidth. Existing map demand assumptions remain provisional: openings × 24 tiles for low/expected and × 60 for heavy exploration; changed viewport dimensions must be measured. These are not public OSM quotas.
+
+Primary sources checked October 17, 2026: Google Fonts' [Newsreader metadata](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/fonts/main/ofl/newsreader/METADATA.pb) identifies Vietnamese coverage and OFL licensing; [Be Vietnam Pro metadata](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/fonts/main/ofl/bevietnampro/METADATA.pb) identifies its OFL licensing. The [Newsreader license](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/fonts/main/ofl/newsreader/OFL.txt) and [Be Vietnam Pro license](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/fonts/main/ofl/bevietnampro/OFL.txt) permit embedding/redistribution subject to retaining notices and license conditions, rather than a recurring font fee. Confirm the selected files' Vietnamese glyphs visually before release. No geographic delivery guarantee is inferred from a font license.
+
+The [OSM tile policy](https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/tiles/) was rechecked October 17, 2026: the configured HTTPS endpoint is correct; web requests need an accurate Referer, browser identification, ordinary caching and visible attribution. Public tiles offer no SLA or guaranteed numeric allowance; access can be blocked without notice, not automatically upgraded for a fee. A provider change requires a verified production failure/eligibility assessment and a refreshed quote; earlier paid prices in this document are not newly verified by this proposal. Inspect deployment headers, CSP and actual failed requests before choosing a remedy. Do not hide errors or use the illustrated fallback as the claimed fix.
+
+**Alternatives and controls:** keeping the current design avoids new asset work but retains its editorial weaknesses. Restyling with the current fonts and owner-supplied covers is simpler and avoids font acquisition; the proposed licensed fonts better support the original identity. A paid tile service can improve contractual reliability but adds supplier dependence and needs current commercial/Vietnam eligibility, quotas and overage checks before selection. Monitor tile failures/request counts and delivered asset bytes; configure supported host/provider budget alerts and spending controls if available. Alerts are not hard caps. Preserve destination links and honest retry/error messaging during outages; the live map remains required.
+
+Only this cost record is updated at the approval stage. PDF regeneration/layout inspection remain pending under the instance resource policy; the existing PDF is already stale and must not be shared as this proposal. On a capable machine run `uv run --with reportlab python scripts/build-billing-brief.py`, then `pdftoppm -scale-to 1400 -png output/pdf/ha-long-billing-brief.pdf /tmp/ha-noi-billing` and visually inspect every page. Recalculate transfer and refresh supplier pricing before release or a paid commitment.
+
 ## Recommended decision
 
 Keep the current free setup for private proof-of-concept reviews. For a public commercial launch, shortlist Stadia Maps Starter for maps, or MapTiler Flex as an alternative. If directions must work inside our site, budget a routing-capable service separately; this brief models Stadia Standard. Check Vietnam map quality, route quality, provider eligibility, and support requirements before selecting a supplier.
